  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLatest satellite and surface observations show freezing fog has\ndeveloped along the CT River, which may persist into the \nmorning commute to both cause slick surfaces/roadways and \nvisibility reductions. Once this clears out, it will be a mostly\nsunny day with highs in the 50s south of the mountains. \n\nBroad upper ridging will then remain the norm through the rest \nof the week and perhaps into the weekend. This will portend to \ndry weather and worsening drought conditions. Highs will average\nabove normal with cool nights due to radiational cooling.\n\nA glance past this weekend, the eventual retreat of high \npressure could feature a window of storminess. While multiple \nmodels suggest this possibility, they also differ in time and \nlocation. This looks to be the phasing, or lack thereof, of a \ncoastal low moving up the Eastern Seaboard with approaching \ntrough from the west. Will monitor this period for the next\nchance of notable precipitation in the region, but can also see\nsome solutions where precip remains light.",
